"Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors (https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.10404)\nciteturn2academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Many idealized problems in signal processing, machine learning and statistics can be reduced to the problem of finding the symmetric canonical decomposition of an underlying symmetric and orthogonally decomposable (SOD) tensor. ... This article shows that even in the presence of perturbation, SROA can still robustly recover the symmetric canonical decomposition of the underlying tensor.\nTitle: Successive Rank-One Approximations for Nearly Orthogonally Decomposable Symmetric Tensors\nAuthors: Cun Mu, Daniel Hsu, Donald Goldfarb\nDate: Mon May 29 21:37:32 2017\n\nMany idealized problems in signal processing, machine learning and statistics can be reduced to the problem of finding the symmetric canonical decomposition of an underlying symmetric and orthogonally decomposable (SOD) tensor. Drawing inspiration from the matrix case, the successive rank-one approximations (SROA) scheme has been proposed and shown to yield this tensor decomposition exactly, and a plethora of numerical methods have thus been developed for the tensor rank-one approximation problem. In practice, however, the inevitable errors (say) from estimation, computation, and modeling necessitate that the input tensor can only be assumed to be a nearly SOD tensor---i.e., a symmetric tensor slightly perturbed from the underlying SOD tensor. This article shows that even in the presence of perturbation, SROA can still robustly recover the symmetric canonical decomposition of the underlying tensor. It is shown that when the perturbation error is small enough, the approximation errors do not accumulate with the iteration number. Numerical results are presented to support the theoretical findings.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConvex Geometry in Florence (https://www.cgf2024.unifi.it/upload/sub/notizie/book_of_abstracts_cgeom_unifi.pdf)\nciteturn2search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Title: Stability of inequalities concerning the mean widthAbstract: The regular simplex maximizes the mean width among convex bodies whose John ... Kipp, Christian (Technical University of Berlin, Germany) ... for polytopal maximizers of the isotropic constant.\nrandom polygons in various probability models in and around a smooth convex disc. The common\nelement in these models is that the generalized random polygons are formed by the intersection\nof all translates of a suitable fixed convex disc containing a sample of i.i.d. random points. Upper\nbounds on variances lead to strong laws of large numbers, lower bounds may be used to prove\ncentral limit theorems. The talk is based on joint works with F. Fodor (University of Szeged) and\nV. V´ıgh (University of Szeged).\nHofst¨atter, Georg (Technical University of Wien, Austria)\nTitle: Convex valuations from Whitney to Nash\nAbstract: We ask the following Whitney-type question for valuations, formulated in terms of\nmixed volumes: suppose that a mixed volume (considered as functional in k open spots) is given\nin every linear subspace of a fixed dimension; can this family be represented as restrictions of a\nlinear combination of mixed volumes (with k open spots) in the total space? Clearly, compatibility\non pairwise intersections is a necessary condition. In this talk, we will show that, together with a\nregularity condition, this is also sufficient when all mixed volumes are smooth.\nThis is joint work with D. Faifman.\nKipp, Christian (Technical University of Berlin, Germany)\nTitle: Small perturbations of polytopes\nAbstract: We study the question how a convex body can be deformed “infinitesimally” such that\nthe resulting shape is still convex. Using an abstract approach motivated by certain first-order\noptimality conditions, we recover a notion of weak derivatives of convex-body-valued functions\ndue to G. Pflug and H. Weisshaupt. We characterize the set of realizable perturbations in the\ncase where the perturbed body is a polytope. As an application, we discuss a necessary condition\nfor polytopal maximizers of the isotropic constant.\nPage 2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPerturbation bounds for (nearly) orthogonally decomposable tensors with statistical applications | Information and Inference: A Journal of the IMA | Oxford Academic (https://academic.oup.com/imaiai/article/12/2/1044/6966017)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Doing so not only provides universal perturbation bounds that can be useful for all these applications together, but also allows us to recognize the fundamental similarities and differences between matrices and higher order tensor from yet another perspective. ... See, e.g. [17, 28, 29] for further discussion of orthogonally decomposable tensors. ... For this property, these vectors are also called robust singular vectors in the literature (see 1]. ... Greedy approaches to symmetric orthogonal tensor decomposition ... Orthogonal decomposition of symmetric tensors ... Singular vectors of orthogonally decomposable tensors\nPerturbation theory of this nature is well-developed in the case of matrices (⁠|$p=2$|⁠) and can be traced back to the classical works [33], [11] and [32]. See, e.g., [30] for a comprehensive survey. These results provide the essential tools for numerous applications in various scientific and engineering domains. As multilinear arrays appear more and more often in these applications, many attempts have been made to develop similar tools for higher order tensors in recent years. Because of the unique challenges associated with higher order tensors, most if not all existing studies in this direction customize their analysis and hence the resulting bounds for a specific algorithm or method. See, e.g., [1, 5, 23, 24]. The aim of this article is to fill in the important step of providing universal perturbation bounds that is in the same spirit as matrix perturbation analysis and independent of a specific algorithm. Doing so not only provides universal perturbation bounds that can be useful for all these applications together, but also allows us to recognize the fundamental similarities and differences between matrices and higher order tensor from yet another perspective.\nFor this property, these vectors are also called robust singular vectors in the literature (see 1]. Interested readers are referred to [5] for further details.\n\nThe argument presented in [5] relies heavily on the hidden convexity of |$F$|⁠, which no longer holds when |$\\mathscr{T}$| is not symmetric. However, their main observations remain valid for general odeco tensors. More specifically, write\n\n$$ \\begin{align}& F({\\textbf{a}}^{(1)},\\ldots, {\\textbf{a}}^{(p)}):=|\\langle \\mathscr{T}, {\\textbf{a}}^{(1)}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes{\\textbf{a}}^{(p)}\\rangle| \\end{align} $$\n\n(4.1)\n\nwith slight abuse of notation. Denote by\n\n$$ \\begin{align}& G({\\textbf{a}}^{(1)},\\ldots, {\\textbf{a}}^{(p)}):=\\left({\\mathscr{T}\\times_2{\\textbf{a}}^{(2)}\\cdots\\times_p{\\textbf{a}}^{(p)}\\over\\|\\mathscr{T}\\times_2{\\textbf{a}}^{(2)}\\cdots\\times_p{\\textbf{a}}^{(p)}\\|},\\ldots,{\\mathscr{T}\\times_1{\\textbf{a}}^{(1)}\\cdots\\times_{p-1}{\\textbf{a}}^{(p-1)}\\over\\|\\mathscr{T}\\times_1{\\textbf{a}}^{(1)}\\cdots\\times_{p-1}{\\textbf{a}}^{(p-1)}\\|}\\right). \\end{align} $$\n\n(4.2)\n\nthe gradient iteration function for |$F$| so that\n\n$$ \\begin{align*} &G_n=\\underbrace{G\\circ G\\circ\\cdots\\circ G}_{n{\\text{ times}}} \\end{align*} $$\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA survey of some tensor analysis techniques for biological systems - Yahyanejad - 2019 - Quantitative Biology - Wiley Online Library (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1007/s40484-019-0186-5)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; An orthogonal decomposition of a symmetric tensor of order k is a decomposition ... A tensor is called orthogonally decomposable if it has an orthogonal decomposition.\nAn orthogonal decomposition of a symmetric tensor of order k is a decomposition\n\n(2)\n\nsuch that the vectors form an orthonormal family of vectors. A tensor is called orthogonally decomposable if it has an orthogonal decomposition. Note that for an orthogonal decomposition .\n\nThe following point is worth mentioning about the decomposition and the -rank. Consider the matrix factoring A = XY^{T} of a matrix A that plays a crucial role in many matrix analysis methods. Using the standard S VD of matrix A, we can easily write A = U∑V^{T} = X Y^{T} where X = U∑ and Y = V. However, the factoring is hardly unique since we can also take X = U∑Z and Y = V Z for any orthogonal matrix Z. In contrast, the factoring of a higher mode tensor is unique under less stringent conditions. For example, in [7] it is shown that there is any symmetric tensor of order 3 has a unique decomposition provided we only allows the case when all the vectors in the decomposition are mutually linearly independent, and such a decomposition can in fact be found in polynomial time. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStability of Simplex Slicing | Discrete & Computational Geometry | Springer Nature Link (https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00454-025-00758-x)\nciteturn2search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In particular, since \\(\\sqrt{\\delta (a)} \\le \\sqrt{2}\\), we have \\(p_a(0) \\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} - 2\\cdot 10^{-5}\\sqrt{\\delta (a)}\\), for every unit vector a as in Theorem 1.As for the stability of cube slicing (see (5) in [13]), the deficit term is of the linear order in the Euclidean distance \\(\\sqrt{\\delta (a)}\\)